  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 215 PM EDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nOverall pattern sets up with the area east of the high pressure \nridge over the southern Plains and the trough over the northeast \ncoast. This will put the area in a more northwest/northerly flow \nregime into the start of the week. For Tuesday afternoon into the \novernight, SPC has placed portions of northeast Georgia in a slight \nrisk for severe weather as another potential MCS moves into the \narea. This one will have a bit more backing from the front in the \narea that begins to push south into Wednesday. This is within the \nRRFS and NAM window which are showing uncertainty on how far south \nthe extent of the severe risk will track. This will be something to \nwatch and unfortunately is dependent on how any activity Monday and \nTuesday morning evolve. \n\nInto mid-week the ridge begins to push back to the west and break \ndown a tad pushing our area into a more dry pattern into Friday but \nwith another trough/low pressure system pushing into the weekend \nrain chances again will return Saturday and Sunday. \n\nTemperatures Tuesday will be elevated with heat index values in the \n105-108 range. Will likely need a heat advisory on Tuesday but \nwaiting to see how lingering convection/MCSs affect the temperatures \non Tuesday like Monday. These elevated temperatures though will be \nconducive for creating an unstable environment ahead of any storm \npotential Tuesday evening.",
